SOC205 - Malicious Macro has been executed |MEDIUM|

Summary  
On Feb 28, 2024 at 08:42 AM UTC, the MSOC team received an alert titled “Malicious Macro Has Been Executed.” The alert was triggered when user Jayne on host 172.16.17[.]198 downloaded a zip file named edit1‑invoice.docm.zip through Explorer (explorer.exe). OSINT analysis confirmed the file to be malicious.

Inside the ZIP was a macro‑enabled Word document (.docm). When opened, the document executed a PowerShell command designed to download and run a file named messbox.exe. Most security vendors classify messbox.exe as malware.

The malicious macro attempted to download the payload using a GET request to:  
http://www.greyhathacker[.]net/tools/messbox.exe

This domain resolved to the IP address 92.204.221.16, which was reported 20 times and confirmed to be malicious. The host attempted to communicate with this IP immediately after the PowerShell execution, indicating successful malware activity.

The affected host was isolated from the network, malicious processes were terminated, and outbound communication to the identified malicious IP and domain was blocked.

Malicious files were removed, a full Defender scan was initiated, persistence checks were performed, and the device was prepared for safe reintegration into the network.



